1. FIX BLURRY IMAGES AND TEXT
==================================================


PROBLEM:
Images and text throughout the website appear blurry, pixelated, faded, or insufficiently sharp.


OBJECTIVE:
Make all images crisp, bright, high-resolution, and visually professional. Ensure all text is sharp and readable.


IMAGE QUALITY:


1. Inspect every image used across the website, including:
   - Homepage hero image.
   - Property category images.
   - Featured property images.
   - Estate development images.
   - Interior photographs.
   - CEO and property manager photographs.
   - Footer background images.
   - Property detail galleries.


2. Replace low-resolution or visibly pixelated images with suitable high-resolution alternatives.


3. Use image sources with a width of at least 1600 pixels for large hero and full-width images whenever available.


4. For property cards and smaller images, use appropriately sized, high-quality source images that provide sufficient resolution for their rendered dimensions.


5. Do not enlarge small, low-resolution images to make them appear high-resolution.


6. Do not stretch or distort images.


7. Preserve the existing image subjects and visual direction wherever possible. Replace an image only when its quality is inadequate or the source is unsuitable.


8. Prefer reliable image sources and verify that replacement URLs load successfully.


9. If the website already has uploaded property photographs, preserve those assets when their quality is sufficient.


IMAGE RENDERING:


Use appropriate width and height values.


For standard photographic cards, use:


width: 100%;
height: 100%;
object-fit: cover;
object-position: center;


Use an appropriate aspect ratio for each image container.


For hero images, adjust object-position as necessary to keep the important architectural features visible.


Do not apply these cover rules indiscriminately to logos, icons, or images that must remain completely visible.


Remove unnecessary image effects, including:


- filter: blur()
- backdrop-filter
- Unnecessary opacity reductions.
- Excessive translucent overlays.
- CSS effects that soften the actual image.
- Transformations that cause visible pixelation.


Use the following subtle image enhancement only when appropriate:


filter: brightness(1.05) contrast(1.05) saturate(1.1);


Do not apply this filter repeatedly or stack multiple image filters.


If an image already has excellent colour and contrast, preserve its natural appearance instead of making it excessively bright.


TEXT CLARITY:


Ensure headings, paragraphs, labels, navigation links, and buttons render sharply.


Use:


-webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;


Use solid, readable text colours consistent with the existing green-and-gold design.


Remove unintended text transparency, blur, and unnecessary filter effects.


Do not change the website's established font family or redesign the typography.


Ensure sufficient contrast between text and its background.


IMAGE LOADING:


For the main above-the-fold hero image, use eager loading and appropriate fetch priority.


Example:


<img
  src="HIGH_RESOLUTION_IMAGE_URL"
  srcset="RESPONSIVE_IMAGE_SOURCES"
  sizes="100vw"
  loading="eager"
  fetchpriority="high"
  alt="Descriptive property image"
/>


Replace the example placeholders with real image URLs and valid responsive image sources.


For below-the-fold images, use lazy loading where appropriate.


Implement responsive srcset and sizes values when multiple suitable image sizes are available.


Do not create fake srcset entries that point to the same low-resolution image.


Preserve the existing image layout, colours, and content.


