2. CHANGE FEATURED PROPERTIES INTO A FADE SLIDESHOW
==================================================


PROBLEM:
The Featured Properties section currently displays multiple property cards side by side or stacked, and its presentation needs to be replaced with a proper slideshow.


OBJECTIVE:
Display exactly ONE featured property card at a time.


Do not display multiple featured property cards simultaneously.


Do not place the slides side by side.


Do not stack the slides vertically.


SLIDESHOW STRUCTURE:


Use the existing Featured Properties section.


Keep its current green-and-gold design, section heading, introductory text, property information, and overall visual identity.


Create a single slideshow viewport containing all the existing featured property slides.


Position the slides absolutely within a relatively positioned viewport so they occupy the same space.


Only the active slide should be visible and interactive.


Inactive slides must not be visible or intercept pointer interactions.


Each slide should contain the appropriate property image and its existing information.


Do not delete the existing property records.


SLIDE TRANSITIONS:


Use a smooth crossfade transition.


When moving to another property:


- The outgoing slide fades out.
- The incoming slide fades in.
- The transition lasts approximately 1 second.
- Both slides remain aligned in the same viewport.
- No horizontal scrolling occurs during the transition.
- No slide should jump vertically or change the section height unexpectedly.


Use opacity transitions rather than a horizontal sliding animation.


Suggested CSS:


.slideshow-viewport {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 100%;
  overflow: hidden;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}


.property-slide {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  opacity: 0;
  visibility: hidden;
  pointer-events: none;
  transition: opacity 1s ease;
}


.property-slide.is-active {
  position: relative;
  opacity: 1;
  visibility: visible;
  pointer-events: auto;
}


Adapt these styles to the existing component structure.


Ensure that the slideshow viewport maintains a stable height based on the active slide.


AUTOPLAY:


Automatically advance to the next property every 8 seconds.


The timer must reset appropriately after manual navigation.


Do not start multiple competing autoplay timers.


NAVIGATION CONTROLS:


Preserve and implement all the following controls:


1. Previous arrow.
2. Next arrow.
3. Slide indicator dots.
4. Play button.
5. Pause button.


FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS:


- Clicking Previous displays the preceding property.
- Clicking Next displays the following property.
- Clicking a dot opens the corresponding property.
- The active dot always matches the displayed property.
- The play button starts autoplay.
- The pause button stops autoplay.
- Autoplay advances through the existing property list.
- After the final property, the slideshow returns to the first property.
- Manual navigation updates the active slide and indicators immediately.
- Manual navigation must not create duplicate timers.
- The controls must remain functional on desktop and mobile.


HOVER AND TOUCH BEHAVIOUR:


Pause autoplay when the user hovers over the slideshow on desktop.


Resume autoplay when the pointer leaves, provided the user has not explicitly paused it.


On mobile, pause autoplay while the user is actively touching or interacting with the slideshow.


Resume autoplay after the interaction ends, provided the user has not explicitly paused it.


Distinguish temporary interaction pauses from an intentional pause using the pause button.


Respect the user's reduced-motion accessibility preference.


Do not remove the existing property details or enquiry buttons.


